---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="About Small Internet"
  description="A free, portable Astro theme with three complete looks, editable Markdown, RSS, and optional Spacefast hosting."
>
  <article class="article">
    <p class="eyebrow">Your own place on the internet</p>
    <header>
      <h1>A weird little home on the web.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Small Internet is a free Astro theme for the things you make, notice,
        and want to keep.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Start with a homepage, a few notes, a shelf of favorite things, and a
        garden of good links. Add to it when you have something to say. It can
        stay small.
      </p>
      <h2>Three looks. One home.</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Personal Homepage</strong> pairs lavender paper with a
        coffee-drinking computer. <strong>Maker’s Notebook</strong> puts your
        projects on a yellow graph-paper desk with a very opinionated folder.
        <strong>Link Garden</strong> grows mint-green link beds beside a plant
        computer and a skeptical snail. Try the buttons above; your choice stays
        on this device.
      </p>
      <h2>Made to be yours</h2>
      <p>
        The source is an ordinary Astro project. Notes, projects, and collection
        items live in Markdown. Your name, links, and now page have simple
        editable files. There is no required subscription, analytics account, or
        CMS.
      </p>
      <p>
        The guestbook can show messages you choose to publish. Its draft feature
        saves only on the visitor’s device; it has no server submission service.
      </p>
      <h2>Get started</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/nickhamze/small-internet">
          Get Small Internet on GitHub ↗
        </a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Open the downloaded project in your editor. With Node 22.12 or newer,
        install the locked dependencies and start the local preview:
      </p>
      <pre><code>npm ci
npm run dev</code></pre>
      <p>
        Change your identity in <code>src/data/site.ts</code>, write in{' '}
        <code>src/content/</code>, and build with <code>npm run build</code>.
        The finished static site is in <code>dist/</code>. The included README
        covers the details.
      </p>
      <div class="theme-callout">
        <h2>Make yours with Spacefast</h2>
        <p>
          Spacefast is an optional host for your Astro site. You can also take
          this theme to another static host.
        </p>
        <p>
          Spacefast’s supported path is to build your site and publish the
          output with its CLI:
        </p>
        <pre><code>npm run build
sf publish ./dist --prebuilt --dry-run
sf publish ./dist --prebuilt</code></pre>
        <p>
          Review the target before publishing. The publish command creates or
          updates a hosted site; it is not a local preview. Turn on the
          Spacefast hosting credit only when your site is actually hosted there.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="https://spacefast.com/docs/recipes/astro/">
            Read Spacefast’s Astro setup guide ↗
          </a>{' '}
          · <a href="https://spacefast.com/">Explore Spacefast ↗</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <h2>Free to keep, free to change</h2>
      <p>
        Small Internet’s code and starting content are available under the MIT
        license. The bundled fonts use their SIL Open Font Licenses. Spacefast’s
        wordmark belongs to Spacefast. The footer links to the theme’s maker;
        the optional hosting banner credits Spacefast only on sites actually
        hosted there.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="article-end">
      <a href="/">Back to this little corner →</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
